<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#burushaski">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burushaski</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Burushaski language</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burushaski is a language spoken in the Hunza, Nagar and Yasin valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit-baltistan">Gilgit-Baltistan</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. It is a language isolate, with no proven relationship to any other language. Its speakers, the Burusho, formed the population of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of Hunza and Nagar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burushaski.jpg" alt="Burushaski" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThatDohDude · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burushaski_in_Perso-Arabic.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Burushaski is a language isolate spoken by the Burusho people, native to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit-baltistan">Gilgit-Baltistan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and its name in the language means our language. About 120,000 speakers live in Pakistan, in the Hunza, Nagar and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a> districts, the Yasin Valley and the Ishkoman Valley, and a few hundred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Because the two regions where it is spoken are separated by Hunza Valley areas where the Indo-Aryan language <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shina-language">Shina</a> is spoken, it is generally believed to have been spoken over a much wider area in the past.</p><p>The Hunza and Nagar varieties differ only slightly, but the Yasin variety, also called Werchikwar, is much more divergent, and is sometimes considered a distinct language. Its speakers are bilingual in Khowar. The variety spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> has been influenced by Kashmiri, Hindi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Burushaski is an ergative language with a generally subject-object-verb word order. Nouns fall into four classes: male human beings, gods and spirits; female human beings and spirits; animals and countable things; and abstract concepts, fluids and uncountable things. Some words belong to two classes with different meanings, so that báalt means apple when countable and apple tree when uncountable. Words for parts of the body and kinship terms always carry a pronominal prefix, so that one cannot say mother or arm, only my arm or your mother.</p><p>The number system is vigesimal, based on twenty, so that 40 is twice twenty and 60 is three times twenty. The verb is very rich in forms: it has three basic stems, and a finite verb can take up to four prefixes and six suffixes in a system of eleven possible positions. Prefixes can mark whether an action was willing or unwilling, as in the difference between he dived and he sank.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Attempts to link Burushaski with other language families have not been accepted by a majority of linguists. Proposals include the North Caucasian, Kartvelian and Yeniseian languages, a wide Dené-Caucasian grouping, and a relationship with Indo-European. Roger Blench noted in 2008 that the supposed evidence rests on vocabulary that may be better explained by language contact, and that almost all Burushaski farming vocabulary appears to be borrowed from Dardic, Tibeto-Burman and North Caucasian languages.</p><p>Burushaski has been predominantly a spoken language. Allamah Nasiruddin Nasir Hunzai wrote poetry in it in the 1940s and devised an alphabet derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, adding letters for sounds Urdu lacks, and Ghulamuddin Ghulam Hunzai wrote in a modified Arabic script. Hermann Berger created a Latin-based orthography favoured by researchers. A three-volume Burushaski-Urdu Dictionary was compiled in 2006, 2009 and 2013 at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>. Tibetan sources record a Bru zha language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a> valley that appears to have been Burushaski, but no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in it are known to survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burushaski” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hunza's <b>Burushaski</b>-speaking people farmed irrigated terraces and, until the late 19th century, raided caravans on the routes between Ladakh and Yarkand.</blockquote><a href="/state/hunza-state">Hunza State</a></li><li><blockquote>Nagar's people, mostly <b>Burushaski</b> and Shina speakers, are Twelver Shia Muslims.</blockquote><a href="/state/nagar-state">Nagar State</a></li></ul></section>
